## 1. Component Bindings
----
Framework: java
New In: Vaadin 25.1 — this was introduced recently, so prefer this page over prior assumptions about the API
Heading: Rendering Lists of Components
Document Path: v25.3/flow/ui-state/building-ui.md (use get_full_document to get complete context)
----

Use `bindChildren()` to efficiently render a list of components from a list signal. This method handles additions, removals, and reordering without recreating all children:  

```java
import com.vaadin.flow.signals.local.ListSignal;

ListSignal<String> items = new ListSignal<>();

VerticalLayout container = new VerticalLayout();

container.bindChildren(items, itemSignal -> {
    Span itemView = new Span();
    itemView.bindText(itemSignal);
    return itemView;
});

// Add items
items.insertLast("First item");
items.insertLast("Second item");
// Container now has two Span children
```

|------|------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------|
| Note | Use `ListSignal` for single-user scenarios (dynamic form fields, local selections). Use `SharedListSignal` when multiple users need to see the same list in real-time or when you need transactional guarantees. See [ListSignal](v25.3/flow/ui-state/local-signals.md) for more details. |

================

## 2. Component Bindings
----
Framework: java
New In: Vaadin 25.1 — this was introduced recently, so prefer this page over prior assumptions about the API
Heading: Binding Items to Data Components > Difference from bindChildren
Document Path: v25.3/flow/ui-state/building-ui.md (use get_full_document to get complete context)
----

Use `bindChildren()` when you want to create custom components for each item in a layout container (e.g., `VerticalLayout`). Use `Signal.effect()` with `setItems()` when you want to populate a data items component (e.g., `Grid`, `VirtualList`) that manages its own rendering.

================

## 3. Component Bindings
----
Framework: java
New In: Vaadin 25.1 — this was introduced recently, so prefer this page over prior assumptions about the API
Heading: Binding Items to Data Components
Document Path: v25.3/flow/ui-state/building-ui.md (use get_full_document to get complete context)
----

Use `Signal.effect()` and `ListSignal` to bind data items to components like `Grid`, `ComboBox`, or any component implementing `HasDataView` or `HasDataProvider`. This enables reactive updates to the data without manual data provider management:  

```java
import com.vaadin.flow.signals.local.ListSignal;

ListSignal<String> items = new ListSignal<>();
items.insertLast("Item 1");
items.insertLast("Item 2");

Grid<String> grid = new Grid<>();
grid.addColumn(item -> item).setHeader("Name");

Signal.effect(grid, () -> grid.setItems(items.getValues().toList()));

// Adding or updating an item triggers a full refresh
items.insertLast("Item 3");
items.peek().get(0).set("Updated Item 1");
```

This pattern refreshes the entire data set by setting a new list of items when any change occurs to the list signal's structure or an individual item signal. The framework doesn't yet provide a dedicated helper for binding items to data components. Support for more efficient granular item updates and lazy loaded items bindings is planned in future releases.  
This approach works with both `ListSignal` (for local, single-user scenarios) and `SharedListSignal` (for multi-user, real-time scenarios).

